  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 236 PM EST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\n***Sunday Night Through Tuesday Night***\n\nTonight, temperatures will cool into the mid to low 30s with light \nwinds and clear skies. Monday morning will be another chilly one, \nhowever temperatures are not expected to drop below freezing. A \nFrost Advisory has been issued and is in effect for tonight through \n10am EST on Monday. The combination of calm to light winds and clear \nskies will lead to radiational cooling, and is expected to develop \nriver valley fog again tonight. \n\nAs high pressure and northwest synoptic flow continues, near normal \ntemperatures and dry conditions will continue for the first part of \nthe work week. High temperatures on Monday will warm in the mid 60s \nacross the area. Nighttime lows are expected to be slightly warmer \nin the upper 30s and low 40s. REFS ensemble guidance has a less than \n10% chance of minimum temperatures cooling below 35 F. On Tuesday \nsouthwesterly flow will return to the Ohio Valley as a shortwave \ndisturbances passes to the south of KY. This will bring slightly \nwarmer temperatures for the afternoon with highs in the upper 60s. \nAreas west of I-65 in south central KY will have the best chance (30-\n40%) of reaching 70 degrees. \n\n***Wednesday Through Saturday***\n\nBy mid-week, the subtropical and polar jets will begin to interact \nwith one another and phase together. This will result in \ncyclogenesis and formation of a low pressure system over in the \nregion. As the system shifts east, precipitation chances will return \nto the forecast. Although this system has the potential to be fairly \nstrong, a shallow moisture profile and low PWAT values will limit \nthe amount of rainfall we see. Although, the placement and \ntrajectory of the system will play a big part into the QPF values. \nCurrent ensemble guidance has been consistent with widespread 0.25\"-\n0.5\". As the associated frontal boundary pushes through the region \non Thursday, a cold airmass will build into the Ohio Valley bringing \ndecreasing temperatures. Nighttime lows on Thursday will hover \naround freezing across the area, with Friday highs in the upper 40s. \nIf enough moisture sticks around Thursday night into Friday morning, \nareas along and east of I-75 could see their first flurries of the \nseason.\n\nOn Saturday, an upper-level ridge off to the west will begin to \nbuild in behind the low pressure system. This will return surface \nhigh pressure to the Ohio Valley, bringing dry conditions back for \nthe weekend. Temperatures on Saturday are expected to warm into the \nupper 40s and low 50s with nighttime lows likely below freezing.",
